            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous ovarian hyperstimulation in a pregnant woman with hypothyroidism.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>705.e1-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This report describes an unusual case in which a naturally conceived pregnancy is associated with spontaneous ovarian hyperstimulation and hypothyroidism.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University medical center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 30-year-old pregnant woman with abdominal pain and distension caused by ovarian hyperstimulation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Medical management and laparotomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Incomplete regression after 3 months.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Thyroid-stimulating hormone level was elevated. Hormonal studies confirmed hypothyroidism with spontaneous ovarian hyperstimulation. She was given levothyroxine 200 mug/d. Results of the hormonal tests for thyroid function were normal 3 months after treatment, but in this case the ovarian cysts did not regress completely until delivery. Laparotomy and cesarean section were done for both diagnosis and treatment. Ten weeks after delivery ovarian cysts regressed completely.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Thyroid hormone replacement seems to be the best therapeutic approach in some patients, but, in some, complete resolution of the ovarian cysts does not occur after 3 months.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
